meta {
  name: book-demo
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/public/v1/demo-requests
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Ravi Menon",
    "restaurant_name": "Coastal Curry",
    "phone": "+91 98765 43210",
    "email": "ravi@coastalcurry.test"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh number per run, so re-running the collection exercises the CREATED path rather than
  // colliding with the row the previous run left behind. Ten digits opening 9, which is what the
  // server's normaliser accepts.
  const suffix = String(Date.now()).slice(-9);
  bru.setVar("demo_phone", "9" + suffix);
  const body = JSON.parse(req.getBody());
  body.phone = bru.getVar("demo_phone");
  req.setBody(body);
}

tests {
  test("responds 201", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(201)
  })

  test("returns a prefixed reference for the lead", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.uid).to.be.a("string").and.match(/^dmo_/)
    expect(res.body.data.requested_at).to.be.a("string").and.not.empty
  })

  test("a second request against the same number is refused with TX_DEM_001", async function () {
    const again = await bru.sendRequest({
      method: "POST",
      url: bru.getEnvVar("base_url") + "/api/public/v1/demo-requests",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      data: {
        name: "Ravi Menon",
        restaurant_name: "Coastal Curry",
        // Same number, written the way a person would write it the second time. It must collide:
        // uniqueness is over the NORMALISED digits, not the string that was typed.
        phone: "0" + bru.getVar("demo_phone"),
        email: "ravi@coastalcurry.test"
      },
      validateStatus: () => true
    })
    expect(again.status).to.equal(409)
    expect(again.data.code).to.equal("TX_DEM_001")
  })
}

docs {
  # Book a demo

Backs the "Book a demo" form on the public landing page (`apps/diner`, marketing route group). No
credentials -- the caller is a restaurant owner who is not a tenant yet, which is the whole reason
the row exists.

## One demo per phone number

The number is normalised before anything is compared or stored: separators come off, and so do a
leading `+91` or `0`. `+91 98765 43210`, `098765 43210` and `9876543210` are one lead, not three.

A repeat submission answers **409 `TX_DEM_001`**, not an error page -- an owner filling the form in
twice has usually just missed the first confirmation, so the landing page renders it as
reassurance. The rule is held by a `UNIQUE` constraint on the normalised column, not only by the
service's pre-check, so two simultaneous submissions of the same number also land on the 409.

## Other answers

| Status | Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 201 | `00000` | Recorded. `data.uid` is the reference an operator quotes on the callback. |
| 409 | `TX_DEM_001` | This number has already booked. |
| 422 | `TX_DEM_002` | Not a ten-digit Indian mobile. |
| 400 | `TX_COM_001` | Name or restaurant name was blank. |

## The email

A successful booking emails `TABLEX_DEMO_NOTIFY_EMAIL`, with the owner as `Reply-To`. It is sent
**after** the row is committed and **off** the request, so a mail outage costs the notification and
never the lead -- the response is 201 either way. A deployment with no `TABLEX_BREVO_API_KEY` logs
that it skipped the send and is otherwise unaffected, which is the ordinary state locally.
}
